          "The strategic question for any candidate in Hi-SD-2 is whether the current 70-26 margin represents a coalition that can be expanded, or whether it reflects a ceiling—the maximum Democratic performance in a district with this demographic composition. A 26% Republican share in a district with 30% white population suggests that roughly 85-90% of white voters are supporting the Republican candidate, which is consistent with partisan polarization at or near maximum compression. Meanwhile, the Asian (16.19%) and Latino (14.96%) communities are delivering roughly 65-75% of their votes to the Democratic candidate—substantial but not unanimous. The small African American population (0.91%) and the ~36.7% demographic gap (the remaining population, likely Other or multiracial) make these categories too small to move the overall margin meaningfully. For Buenaventura to hold or expand this seat, the strategy must be consolidation of the existing Asian and Latino coalition while maintaining sufficient white support to prevent erosion—not building new constituencies, but ensuring the coalition that delivered 70% does not fracture. Any Republican challenger would need to both depress Asian and Latino turnout and expand the white Republican share simultaneously, a mathematically steep climb in a district this blue."
