          "The 67-33 Democratic margin in 2022 is robust but not unbreakable, and it reflects specific alignments rather than inevitable Democratic dominance. The $84,238 median household income places this district in the upper-middle range for urban California, and the 43.7% college attainment is substantial but not dominant — meaning nearly 60% of adults lack a four-year degree and may have different economic concerns than the college-educated minority. The 50.8% renter rate is the single most important demographic signal: half the district does not own property, which immediately establishes housing costs, tenant protections, and rental-market regulation as core economic issues, not secondary ones. The 43.9 median age suggests a mix of younger renters establishing themselves and older residents aging in place. For an incumbent or challenger, the strategic question is whether the 67% Democratic coalition is built on durable positions (housing affordability, immigrant economic access, healthcare) or on opposition to a weakened opposition party — a distinction that matters enormously when the political environment shifts."
