          "Hi-SD-15 is a demographically unusual district within California's urban landscape: 38.6% Asian, 23.9% white, 11.9% Hispanic, 5.4% Black, with approximately 20% unaccounted for in the standard Census categories—likely reflecting the district's position as a hub for immigrant communities, mixed-race households, and recent arrivals whose primary identity organizing may not align with Census racial categories. The $91,442 median household income and 31.5% college attainment place this squarely in the affluent urban professional demographic. The 68% Democratic margin in 2022 under Wakai reflects not a stable supermajority but rather the current alignment of an Asian-plurality, college-educated, higher-income urban constituency with Democratic economic and social positioning. This is not a working-class progressive district or a traditional minority-coalition stronghold—it is a constituency of homebuyers, small-business owners, and professional-class immigrants whose economic interests center on housing costs, education quality, business regulation, and tax policy. The 64.3% renter rate, the highest of any district in this sequence, is the critical counterweight to that income figure: renters at $91k income in San Diego are not economically secure despite nominal affluence, and housing affordability is the material issue that will determine whether the 68% Democratic coalition holds or fractures.",
