          { name: "Ignoring or Generic Treatment of the Renter Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "With 53.5% of the district's households renting despite $93,343 median income, the renter constituency is not a special interest or a subset of the broader middle class—it is a majority of the electorate with specific, material policy concerns that a $93k income does not solve (housing cost constraints are real even at higher income levels in expensive markets). A candidate who treats renting as a transitional status rather than a legitimate household type, or who ignores renter-specific policy concerns in favor of generic homeowner-oriented messaging, is ignoring the material concerns of a majority of the district's households. This is a failure both of governance representation and of political strategy." }
